WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.876 --> 00:00:17.818
20 Dear Daddy Longlegs, Sometimes I worry that I'm not a genius.

2
00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:21.620
This usually happens right after I've read a good book.

3
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.101
Will you be disappointed if I don't become a great author?

4
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:32.204
In the spring everything is so green and beautiful.

5
00:00:34.752 --> 00:00:37.514
I feel like turning my back on school and playing outside.

6
00:00:38.935 --> 00:00:41.478
It's much more fun to live life than to write about it.

7
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.901
I feel— "Eek!"

8
00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:49.964
This letter has been interrupted by someone screaming—me!

9
00:00:52.566 --> 00:00:54.528
Sallie immediately banged on my door.

10
00:00:54.548 --> 00:00:56.249
"Are you all right?"

11
00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:58.951
"What's going on?"

12
00:00:59.412 --> 00:01:00.172
Julia cried.

13
00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:02.995
"Why are you screaming?"

14
00:01:04.852 --> 00:01:07.933
I opened my door and pointed to a centipede on my chair.

15
00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:10.074
"That's why!

16
00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:12.794
It fell from the ceiling and landed next to me!"

17
00:01:13.634 --> 00:01:15.335
Julia took a quick look and shuddered.

18
00:01:15.355 --> 00:01:19.416
"Ugh." Then she went back to her room.

19
00:01:20.556 --> 00:01:22.877
Thank goodness for Sallie, who stayed to help me.

20
00:01:25.498 --> 00:01:28.099
She looked around my room and then grabbed my hairbrush.

21
00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:31.359
"Stand back!"

22
00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:36.237
Sallie whacked the centipede with the back of the brush.

23
00:01:36.257 --> 00:01:40.760
(I'll never be able to use that brush again!)

24
00:01:41.961 --> 00:01:46.683
She killed the bug's front end, but its rear fifty feet ran under a chest of drawers.

25
00:01:46.703 --> 00:01:49.445
"It escaped!"

26
00:01:49.905 --> 00:01:52.386
Sallie sounded disappointed that we hadn't caught it.

27
00:01:52.406 --> 00:01:57.029
"This dorm is so old and full of centipedes."

28
00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.370
"You did your best."

29
00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.852
I picked up my cup, which I'd dropped in my rush to escape.

30
00:02:04.036 --> 00:02:05.397
"I hate centipedes!

31
00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:07.998
They're disgusting creatures.

32
00:02:09.358 --> 00:02:11.139
I'd rather find a tiger under my bed!"

33
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.581
The centipede was only one of the annoying things that happened today.

34
00:02:18.562 --> 00:02:21.023
I woke up late because I didn't hear the morning bell.

35
00:02:23.524 --> 00:02:25.365
I broke a shoelace while getting dressed.

36
00:02:25.905 --> 00:02:28.346
I was late for breakfast and my first class.

37
00:02:30.347 --> 00:02:33.588
Then in math class, I had a little argument with the professor.

38
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.781
"I don't understand why my answer is wrong," I said.

39
00:02:37.801 --> 00:02:42.882
"Because you've worked the problem incorrectly," she said.

40
00:02:42.902 --> 00:02:46.703
"But this is how the textbook said to do it!"

41
00:02:47.604 --> 00:02:49.024
I insisted that I was right.

42
00:02:49.044 --> 00:02:52.365
"Look it up again," she said.

43
00:02:52.385 --> 00:02:56.066
"Now, class, let's move on to the next problem."

44
00:02:57.266 --> 00:03:00.407
When I looked in my textbook, I saw she was right.

45
00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:02.048
Of course.

46
00:03:03.692 --> 00:03:05.533
On and on went the annoying things.

47
00:03:06.814 --> 00:03:08.796
For lunch we had gray stew.

48
00:03:10.137 --> 00:03:11.918
It reminded me of orphanage food.

49
00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:17.662
In English the professor gave us an impossible poem to analyze.

50
00:03:18.842 --> 00:03:19.903
No one knew what to write.

51
00:03:21.064 --> 00:03:23.826
So we sat there in silence for 45 minutes.

52
00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:28.449
Then it rained, and we couldn't play golf in gym class.

53
00:03:31.831 --> 00:03:34.653
When I got home, my new spring dress had arrived.

54
00:03:36.793 --> 00:03:39.194
But it was so tight that I couldn't sit down.

55
00:03:39.214 --> 00:03:44.715
Oh, and the maid had cleaned my room while I was at classes.

56
00:03:45.776 --> 00:03:47.456
She mixed up all my papers!

57
00:03:50.277 --> 00:03:52.438
Supper was as delicious as lunch.

58
00:03:53.618 --> 00:03:55.759
In other words, more orphanage food.

59
00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:00.640
I was finally sitting down to read when someone knocked on my door.

60
00:04:01.672 --> 00:04:03.152
It was a girl from Latin class.

61
00:04:03.172 --> 00:04:08.774
"May I ask a quick question about the homework?"

62
00:04:09.154 --> 00:04:09.594
she said.

63
00:04:11.595 --> 00:04:15.396
She stayed for an hour, talking about the most boring things.

64
00:04:17.536 --> 00:04:20.057
After she left, I decided to write to you.

65
00:04:21.237 --> 00:04:22.918
And that's when the centipede arrived.

66
00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:27.519
So I've had a bad day.

67
00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:30.200
Anybody can face a crisis.

68
00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.081
But I think it's harder to deal with life's little annoyances.

69
00:04:35.361 --> 00:04:36.442
That requires spirit.

70
00:04:38.582 --> 00:04:41.263
From now on, I'll try to be that kind of person.

71
00:04:42.343 --> 00:04:45.504
You'll never hear me complain about Julia wearing silk stockings.

72
00:04:46.565 --> 00:04:48.945
Or centipedes dropping in while I'm writing a letter.

73
00:04:50.686 --> 00:05:00.862
Yours truly, Judy May 5 Dear Daddy Longlegs, I just received a most disturbing letter from Mrs.

74
00:05:00.922 --> 00:05:05.606
Lippett. She hopes I am behaving myself and doing well in my studies.

75
00:05:05.626 --> 00:05:08.027
(That's not the disturbing part.)

76
00:05:09.448 --> 00:05:12.951
She wants me to return to the orphanage this summer and work there.

77
00:05:12.971 --> 00:05:15.493
(That's the disturbing part.)

78
00:05:16.573 --> 00:05:20.596
I can imagine what happened at this month's meeting of the trustees and ladies' committee.

79
00:05:22.177 --> 00:05:25.740
Mrs. Lippett stood up and said, "Thank you all for coming today.

80
00:05:26.988 --> 00:05:29.991
We must discuss Jerusha Abbott's future."

81
00:05:31.932 --> 00:05:34.495
Then one of the trustees (maybe you?)

82
00:05:34.755 --> 00:05:36.196
said, "I thought that was settled.

83
00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:37.717
She's studying to be a writer."

84
00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:41.160
Mrs. Lippett looked very respectful.

85
00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.264
"Yes, we did settle that part of her future, sir.

86
00:05:46.285 --> 00:05:47.686
But I'm talking about this summer.

87
00:05:49.207 --> 00:05:51.009
I assume she has no place to go.

88
00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.192
She is, of course, an orphan."

89
00:05:55.824 --> 00:05:57.906
Several of the ladies nodded in agreement.

90
00:05:57.926 --> 00:06:04.070
"So I respectfully suggest that Jerusha be allowed to return here," Mrs.

91
00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:04.790
Lippett went on.

92
00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.952
"There's plenty of work for her to do."

93
00:06:09.413 --> 00:06:11.455
Did anyone speak up to defend me?

94
00:06:12.375 --> 00:06:14.817
Did anyone ask what I thought of this idea?

95
00:06:16.898 --> 00:06:17.659
Obviously not.

96
00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.001
So I'll tell you what I think of Mrs.

97
00:06:21.061 --> 00:06:23.162
Lippett's proposal.

98
00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:25.384
I HATE THE JOHN GRIER HOME.

99
00:06:26.584 --> 00:06:28.446
I would rather die than go back there!

100
00:06:29.607 --> 00:06:30.688
Even for just a summer.

101
00:06:31.708 --> 00:06:34.531
Yours most truthfully, Jerusha Abbott
